#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128773 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "eec994b4-82ac-568c-b493-dc9a21102c2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-11156" :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(u,v) = v\\sin{u}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= -2v\\sin u & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|= 2\\cos u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= v\\cos u & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|= \\sin u\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2v\\sin u & 2\\cos u \\\\ v\\cos u & \\sin u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-2v\\sin u \\cdot \\sin u - 2\\cos u \\cdot v\\cos u \\\\\n&=-2v(\\sin^{2} u + \\cos^{2} u) \\\\\n&=-2v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128795 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "32f92fce-b4b9-5e7a-9fe0-255b15edf312" :question/math-academy-id "e-12417" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $\\Delta$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta{A}$. Find an approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}\\big(\\mathbf{T}(\\Delta)\\big) \\approx \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{Area}(\\Delta)}_{\\large \\delta{A}}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}v^{3} & 3uv^{2} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= v^{3} \\cdot 2v - 3uv^{2} \\cdot 2u \\\\\n&= 2v^{4} - 6u^{2}v^{2} \\\\\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) \\mid _{(-1, 2)} &= (2v^{4} - 6u^{2}v^{2}) \\mid _{u =-1v = 2} \\\\\n&= 2(2)^{4} - 6(-1)^{2}(2)^{2} \\\\\n&= 32 - 24 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nArea(T(Δ))\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx & 8\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot δA; = 8δA\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128796 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/id #uuid "6102567f-7035-5fa2-b12e-80a0470de4a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12418" :question/problem "Sketch the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$\nis (locally) orientation-reversing." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} < 0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}e^{v} & ue^{v} \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{v} \\cdot (-2v) - ue^{v} \\cdot 2u \\\\\n&=-2e^{v}(u^{2} + v)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2e^{v}(u^{2} + v) & < 0 \\\\ u^{2} + v & > 0 \\\\ v & > - u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv=-u^2\n$$\n\ndefines a downward-opening parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1999/Source/Images/c1f0d183af763ad356f1fbb644c7e42e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128941 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d56cec4a-6121-5988-aaea-478fb6048a6d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian Determinant of a Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "28562339-0fe0-5d56-8767-2ec0834525d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6810" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx=(u+v)^3\n$$\n\nand $y=(u-v)^2$.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= 3(u + v)^{2} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|= 3(u + v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= 2(u - v) & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|=-2(u - v)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}3(u + v)^{2} & 3(u + v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u - v) & -2(u - v)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3(u + v)^{2} \\cdot (-2(u - v)) - 3(u + v)^{2} \\cdot 2(u - v) \\\\\n&=-12(u + v)^{2}(u - v)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a70becb4-79f8-5997-94b7-5f28df55f8eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1999/Source/Images/q-160341-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126435} {:answer/id #uuid "d81549fa-8d14-5411-b716-bd6fe31e9c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1999/Source/Images/q-160341-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126436} {:answer/id #uuid "f061e33e-5529-5193-9672-0bc670bf7886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1999/Source/Images/q-160341-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126437} {:answer/id #uuid "3a178425-276d-58c2-ba03-99a817cf8677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1999/Source/Images/q-160341-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126438} {:answer/id #uuid "63be98e5-4a9b-518e-8d73-bfb0258b